  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic reaction network centered on iodine at a surface or interface. Molecular iodine, I₂, sits in the middle and is shown converting into several iodine-containing species. In water, I₂ can form HIO, and HIO then quickly leads to iodate, IO₃⁻, indicated by solid blue arrows for fast steps. A dashed path suggests a slower route connecting I₂ more directly toward iodate.\n\nA parallel bromine-assisted pathway appears on the right. In the presence of Br⁻, I₂ forms IBr, and IBr can reversibly interconvert with iodate in water while releasing Br₂. The red and blue opposing arrows indicate bidirectional behavior, with the legend showing that solid arrows represent faster reactions and dashed arrows slower ones. Vertical arrows above I₂ also suggest an additional exchange or transfer process involving I₂, though the exact label is not fully visible.\n\nOverall, the image compares competing aqueous interfacial pathways for iodine transformation, highlighting that iodine can be oxidized toward iodate either through HIO or through a bromide-dependent intermediate, IBr. The main scientific message is the balance between fast and slow steps in this halogen reaction system.",
